{"reply":"In Chapter 5, the Caterpillar turns identity into an interrogation: “Who are _you?_” When Alice says she cannot explain herself because “I’m not myself,” her confusion becomes something she is expected to account for rather than something the Caterpillar helps resolve. The repeated, calm questions make the exchange feel like a test of whether identity must be stable before it can be named.

Douglas V. Porpora’s essay, [“The Caterpillar’s Question: Contesting Anti-Humanism’s Contestations”](https://doi.org/10.1111/1468-5914.00036), takes that question as a philosophical problem. It argues that difficulty identifying who we are does not necessarily mean there is no coherent person undergoing the experience; the very confusion still belongs to someone. That gives your reading a useful tension: the Caterpillar may be testing Alice’s definition of herself, not simply asking her to report a fact.

